roved. **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Verification Log**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Preliminaries  \n\n> \u201cDefine  \n> \\[\n> T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n> \\]  \n> \u2026 A simple count gives  \n> \\[\n> |T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** The set description is correct; the sum correctly counts the lattice points in the right\u2011angled triangle, yielding \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\). No issue.\n\n> \u201cFor a column \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) the points of \\(T_n\\) \u2026 contain \\(h_c=n+1-c\\) points. In particular  \n> \\[\n> h_n=1,\\qquad h_{n-1}=2,\\qquad h_{n-2}=3 .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** Direct from the definition; correct.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Points on a sunny line  \n\n> \u201cLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \\(T_n\\).  \n> Write its reduced slope as \\(m=\\frac pq\\) \u2026 with \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\). \u2026  \n> Hence \\(x_2-x_1\\) is a multiple of \\(q\\); write \\(x_2-x_1 = q\\,t\\) \u2026  \n> Consequently \\(y_2-y_1 = p\\,t\\).\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** Standard rational\u2011slope argument; correct.\n\n> \u201cNow consider the sum \\(s=x+y\\). \u2026 we have  \n> \\[\n> s_2-s_1 = (p+q)t .\n> \\]  \n> Thus the values of \\(s=x+y\\) taken by integer points of \\(L\\) form an arithmetic progression whose common difference is \\(|p+q|\\).\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** Algebra is correct; the progression statement follows.\n\n> \u201cSince every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\), the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on \\(L\\) is at most  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n>   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n>   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** The inequality follows because \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\rfloor\\)