izontal, one slope\u202f\\(-1\\)) each covering a different point of that column, together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines, to cover all points without any vertical line. The argument that a vertical line is *necessary* is unjustified.\n\n> \u201cConsider the three rightmost columns, whose sizes are given by (2).  \n> If \\(k\\le2\\) then column \\(x=n-2\\) (which contains three points) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; consequently **one of the non\u2011sunny lines must be vertical and must be the line \\(x=n-2\\)**.\u201d\n\n- **Issue 3 (Justification Gap):** The step \u201cmust be the line \\(x=n-2\\)\u201d does not follow. A non\u2011sunny line could be horizontal (e.g., \\(y=1\\)) or of slope\u202f\\(-1\\) and still cover a point of column \\(n-2\\). The argument that the only way to cover the excess point(s) is by a vertical line at exactly that column is unsupported.\n\n> \u201cNow look at column \\(x=n-1\\) (two points).  \n> If we do **not** use a vertical line \\(x=n-1\\), both its points must be covered by sunny lines, i.e. each sunny line must contain exactly one of them.  \n> The remaining column \\(x=n\\) contains a single point \\((n,1)\\).  \n> A line joining \\((n,1)\\) with either of the two points of column \\(n-1\\) is either horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\); both are non\u2011sunny.  Hence \\((n,1)\\) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column \\(n-1\\).  Consequently the point \\((n,1)\\) would require a third sunny line, contradicting the assumption \\(k\\le2\\).\u201d\n\n- **Issue 4 (Justification Gap):** This reasoning depends on the previous (unjustified) claim that a vertical line \\(x=n-2\\) is already forced. Even if we accept that, the argument that a sunny line cannot pass through \\((n,1)\\) and a point of column \\(n-1\\) is correct, but the overall impossibility of \\(k=2\\) is not fully established because alternative configurations (e.g., using a horizontal or slope\u202f\\(-1\\) non\u2011sunny line to cover the extra point in column \\(n-2\\)) have not been excluded. Hence the proof of \u201c\\(k=2\\) is impossible\u201d is incomplete.\n\n> \u201cSince a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having \\(k\\ge4\\) would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most \\(n-4\\) vertical lines to cover the remaining columns.  Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column \\(n-2\\), we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.  Hence **\\(k\\ge4\\) is also impossible**.\u201d\n\n- **Issue 5 (Justification Gap):** The counting argument is vague. It does not rigorously show that the number of vertical lines available (\\(n-k\\)) is insufficient to cover the columns that are not fully handled by sunny lines. A correct impossibility proof would need to consider the exact distribution of points among columns and the maximal number of points a sunny line can cover (which can be up to roughly \\(n/2\\) points, not just one per column). The presented reasoning therefore lacks sufficient justification.\n\n> \u201cCombining the two observations we obtain  \n> \\[\n> k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad k\\neq2,\\;k\\neq4,5,\\dots,\n> \\]  \n> so the only candidates are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** The conclusion matches the correct answer, but it rests on the previous gaps. Since the final answer is correct, the gaps are classified as Justification Gaps, not Critical Errors.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Constructions  \n\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cTake the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). \u2026 every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** Correct; all lines are non\u2011sunny, exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covering all points. No issue.\n\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n> \\[\n> L:\