ns\u202f3 we proved that \\(k\\) can be only \\(0,1\\) or \\(3\\).  \n> Section\u202f4 provides explicit constructions \u2026 therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\u201d\n\n- **Verification:** The constructions cover the three claimed values, and the (imperfect) impossibility arguments do not contradict the final answer. Hence the conclusion is correct.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution arrives at the correct set \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) and supplies valid constructions for each case. However, the impossibility arguments for \\(k=2\\) and for all \\(k\\ge4\\) contain several logical gaps and an inaccurate bound on the number of points a sunny line can contain. These are **Justification Gaps** rather than Critical Errors, because they do not affect the correctness of the final answer."

