            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.  Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through\nthe leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line.  A vertical line\ncovers **all** points of a column, whereas a horizontal line or a line of\nslope \\(-1\\) can meet a column in at most one point.  Hence after the\nreplacement we still cover every point of \\(S_n\\) and we have exactly the same\nnumber \\(k\\) of sunny lines.\n\nConsequently we may (and shall) assume that the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are the\nvertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nAll points with abscissa \\(a\\le n-k\\) are already covered.  The remaining\npoints are\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{1.2}\n\\]\n\na right‑angled integer triangle of side‑length \\(k\\).  Thus the original\nproblem is equivalent to **Problem (*)** stated in the summary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A bound for a sunny line inside \\(T_k\\)\n\nLet a sunny line be given.  Multiplying its equation by a common denominator we\nobtain an integer equation\n\n\\[\np x-q y=c ,\\qquad \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\;q>0,\\;p\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAll integer points on the line are described by (3) above:\n\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0+qt,\\;y_0+pt),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nTheir sums satisfy (4):\n\n\\[\nx+y=(x_0+y_0)+(p+q)t .\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\); therefore the sum changes by at least\n\\(|p+q|\\ge1\\) when \\(t\\) varies by one.\n\nInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) we have \\(2\\le x+y\\le k+1\\).  From (2.3) the\nnumber of admissible integers \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1-2}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1=\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|p+q|=1\\) then \\(p+q=\\pm1\\).  The equality \\(p+q=1\\) forces\n\\(q\\ge2\\) (otherwise \\(p=0\\) which would give a horizontal line).  Because\n\\(x=x_0+qt\\) must stay in the interval \\([1,k]\\), the number of admissible\n\\(t\\) is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor k/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nThe same reasoning works for \\(p+q=-1\\).  Hence (2.4) holds for **every**\nsunny line, and the bound is sharp (see §4).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|\\,\\text{sunny line}\\cap T_k\\,|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Counting argument\n\nThe triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nIf we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, (2.5) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n*Even \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\).  Then the left‑hand side of (3.2) equals\n\\(2m\\cdot m=m(2m)=k^{2}/2\\), whereas the right‑hand side equals\n\\(m(2m+1)=k^{2}/2+m\\).  Because \\(m>0\\), (3.2) is false.  Hence **no even\n\\(k\\ge2\\) is possible**.\n\n*Odd \\(k\\).*  Write \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\)\nand (3.2) becomes an equality:\n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2 .\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently every sunny line must contain **exactly** \\(m+1\\) points of\n\\(T_{2m+1}\\), and the \\(k\\) lines must be pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Sunny lines that attain the bound\n\nEquation (2.1) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities:\n\n* \\(p=q=1\\) (slope \\(1\\));\n* \\(p=-2,\\;q=1\\) (slope \\(-2\\));\n* \\(p=-1,\\;q=2\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).\n\nFor \\(k=2m+1\\) the corresponding lines are exactly the three lines listed in\n(10).  A direct substitution shows that each of them contains \\(m+1\\) points of\n\\(T_{2m+1}\\) and that the three point‑sets are disjoint.  No other sunny line\ncan contain \\(m+1\\) points, because any other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\)\nor \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller\nupper bound.\n\nThus, to cover \\(T_{2m+1}\\) with \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines we must use **only**\nthe three lines (10).  Hence we need \\(2m+1\\le3\\); i.e. \\(k=1\\) or\n\\(k=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and cover\n  every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell:\\; y=2x-2n+1\n  \\]\n\n  passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.\n  Together the \\(n\\) lines satisfy the requirements.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  The uncovered set is \\(T_3\\).  The three sunny lines\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_{1}&: y=x,\\\\\n  L_{2}&: y=-2x+5,\\\\\n  L_{3}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  contain respectively the points  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{l}\n  L_{1}:\\;(1,1),(2,2),\\\\[2pt]\n  L_{2}:\\;(1,3),(2,1),\\\\[2pt]\n  L_{3}:\\;(1,2),(3,1),\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n  which are exactly the six points of \\(T_3\\).  Hence all points of\n  \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines (with exactly \\(k\\) sunny) covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) exists are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  The three families of constructions above show\nthat each of these values is indeed attainable.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-24 Phase 2)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Determine all k such that n lines with exactly k sunny lines cover all required points.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"Column x=n-2 has 3 points, so one of the non-sunny lines **must be vertical**. Therefore k=2 is impossible. Final answer: k∈{0,1,3}.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: k∈{0,1,3} ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Valid constructions provided for k=0,1,3 ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"one of the non-sunny lines must be vertical\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The wording is imprecise; the solution should say \"can be taken to be vertical without loss of generality\" since non-sunny lines could also be horizontal or slope -1. However, the underlying logic (that columns with many points require special handling) is sound, and the final answer k∈{0,1,3} is correct. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do this):**\n*   ~~**Location:** \"must be vertical\"~~\n    *   ~~**Issue:** Critical Error - This claim is false because non-sunny lines could be horizontal.~~ ❌ WRONG - This would be hypercritical; the solution's logic is valid despite imprecise wording.\n\n---\n\n**Example 2: Critical Error (truly invalid)**\n*This example shows a fundamental mathematical error.*\n\nProblem: \"Determine all k...\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For k=2, I tried many constructions and couldn't find one. Therefore k=2 doesn't work. Final answer: k∈{0,1,3}.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: k∈{0,1,3} ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check impossibility reasoning: \"I tried and failed\" ✗ INVALID (falls under EXCEPTION)\n3. Decision: Invalid reasoning → Classify as **Critical Error**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"I tried many constructions and couldn't find one\"\n    *   **Issue:** Critical Error - Failure to find a construction is not a proof of impossibility. The solution provides no rigorous argument (no counting argument, no pigeonhole principle, no proof by contradiction). This falls under the IMPORTANT EXCEPTION in the decision rule: completely invalid reasoning even with correct answer.\n\n---\n\n**Example 3: Presentation Issue with Typo (Justification Gap)**\n\nProblem: \"Determine all k...\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"With |p+q|=2 we get three lines: (p,q)=(1,1), (-2,1), (-1,2). These cover all required points. Final answer: k∈{0,1,3}.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: k∈{0,1,3} ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Three lines are valid and explicitly verified ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct + constructions valid → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification: