e bound \\(|\\text{sunny line}\\cap T_k|\\le\\lfloor(k+1)/2\\rfloor\\), the inequality follows directly. The subsequent parity analysis (even \\(k\\) impossible, odd \\(k\\) forces equality) is algebraically correct. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Identification of sunny lines that attain the bound  \n\n> “Equation (2.1) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities:  \n> \\[\n> p=q=1\\;( \\text{slope }1),\\qquad p=-2,\\;q=1\\;( \\text{slope }-2),\\qquad p=-1,\\;q=2\\;( \\text{slope }-\\tfrac12).\n> \\]”\n\n*Issue.*  \nThe pairs \\((-2,1)\\) and \\((-1,2)\\) satisfy \\(|p+q|=1\\), not 2. Moreover, there are many other coprime pairs with \\(|p+q|=2\\) (e.g. \\((-1,3),\\;(3,-1)\\), etc.) that are omitted. The statement is therefore factually inaccurate.  \n\n*Classification.* **Justification Gap** – a mis‑statement that does not affect the final conclusion but leaves the reasoning incomplete.\n\n> “No other sunny line can contain \\(m+1\\) points, because any other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.”\n\n*Issue.*  \nThe case \\(|p+q|=2\\) with \\(q\\ge3\\) (or \\(p\\ge3\\)) is ignored; such lines indeed give a smaller bound, but the argument does not mention them. Consequently the claim that **only** the three listed lines can achieve the bound is not fully justified.  \n\n*Classification.* **Justification Gap** – the exclusion of the missing cases is an omission in the argument.\n\n> “Thus, to cover \\(T_{2m+1}\\) with \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines we must use **only** the three lines (10).”\n\n*Issue.*  \nThere is no equation numbered (10) in the text; this is a typographical/reference error.  \n\n*Classification.* **Justification Gap** – a presentation slip that does not affect the logical content.\n\nDespite the above gaps, the subsequent reasoning (that only three distinct lines can each contain \\(m+1\\) points, so \\(2m+1\\le3\\) and hence \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=3\\)) is correct because, as 